\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a precise calibration of what randomness is worth to a lossless compressor, and of where efficient compression collides with cryptographic hardness. On the information-theoretic side we prove a double-counting theorem for \emph{Las Vegas} (zero-error, seeded) compression: for any finite seed space $R$, any target budget $s$, and any finite target set $T$, the total number of (object, good seed) incidences is at most $|R|(2^{s+1}-1)$. Consequently a randomized compressor with success probability $\delta$ gains at most $\log_2(1/\delta)+1$ bits over the deterministic pigeonhole ceiling---\emph{independently of the number of random bits consumed}---and a compressor required to succeed for every seed gains exactly nothing. The bound is tight to within one bit, witnessed by an explicit seeded prefix system, and the seed budget obeys a strict hierarchy: with $2^k$ seeds every string of length $k+s$ is compressible to $s$ bits, while with $2^{k-1}$ seeds no seeded family achieves this. A layer-cake inequality upgrades all counting bounds to average-case (Shannon-type) statements: the average description length of a set of $2^n$ objects is at least $n-2$ deterministically and at least $n-k-3$ with $2^k$ seeds, and the constant $2$ is sharp.

On the computational side we fix an abstract class of feasible algorithms closed under guarding, bounded search, and \emph{Las Vegas simulation} (run finitely many seeds, return the first verified output). We prove that a one-way function for the class defeats every Las Vegas algorithm \emph{totally}: there is a value in the range on which all seeds fail simultaneously. This holds for inversion, for exact compression search, and for approximate compression search with arbitrary additive slack; and the existence of a one-way function is \emph{equivalent} to hardness of Las Vegas compression search. Our main new contribution concerns the \emph{decision} task ``does $y$ have a program of length $n$ extending the prefix $w$?'', whose answers carry no certificate. We show that the classical bit-by-bit reconstruction needs the decision oracle to be correct \emph{only at the single string being compressed}, that Las Vegas families of such locally correct oracles derandomize into deterministic inverters, and hence that under a one-way function every seeded decision oracle is wrong at some describable value. Finally we show the obstruction is purely computational: the ideal (non-effective) oracle, fed to the same reconstruction, is an exact shortest-program finder. Randomness helps compression exactly up to the computational-hardness boundary, and no further.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Two walls}

Lossless compression meets two distinct obstacles, and confusing them is the source of most folklore in the area.

The first is \emph{informational} and unconditional. Fix any decoder $D$ mapping programs to objects. The number of objects $y$ admitting a $D$-program of length at most $s$ is at most the number of binary strings of length at most $s$, namely $2^{s+1}-1$. No cleverness, no computation, no oracle changes this. Short descriptions are scarce.

The second obstacle is \emph{computational} and conditional. Scarcity says nothing about \emph{finding} the short description that a given object happens to have. A string may possess a $50$-bit program while every efficient algorithm fails to locate it. This second wall, unlike the first, cannot be established unconditionally with present techniques; it is exactly as strong as the standard assumptions of cryptography.

This paper calibrates both walls in the presence of randomness, and links them.

\subsection{The question}

Randomness is the reflexive answer to a hard search problem. The question splits in two, with strikingly different answers.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item \textbf{How many objects can a randomized compressor handle?} Combinatorics. Answer: a factor $1/\delta$ more than a deterministic one, where $\delta$ is the success probability; equivalently $\log_2(1/\delta)+1$ extra bits. Seed length is irrelevant.
  \item \textbf{Can a randomized compressor \emph{find} short descriptions that a deterministic one cannot?} Complexity theory. Answer: no, provided the ambient class can simulate ``try all seeds and keep the verified answer''.
\end{itemize}

The second answer has an apparent loophole: it depends on outputs being \emph{verifiable}. We test the loophole against the one compression task whose outputs are not verifiable---the decision task---and find that it closes.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
  \item A Las Vegas counting theorem (\S\ref{sec:count}) and its corollaries: the success-probability law, worthlessness of zero-error randomness, tightness within one bit, survival of incompressible strings.
  \item Average-case bounds from pure counting (\S\ref{sec:avg}) via a layer-cake inequality, deterministic and seeded, with a sharpness computation.
  \item A strict seed hierarchy: each random bit is worth exactly one bit of compression.
  \item Total failure of Las Vegas algorithms at the one-way boundary (\S\ref{sec:crypto}), with an equivalence between one-wayness and hardness of Las Vegas compression search.
  \item Search-to-decision under \emph{local} correctness (\S\ref{sec:decision}), the main new technical contribution.
  \item Separation of the informational and computational obstructions (\S\ref{sec:ideal}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout, a \emph{string} is a finite sequence of bits; $|y|$ denotes length, $wp$ denotes concatenation, and $\Bit(n)$ is the set of strings of length exactly $n$, so $|\Bit(n)|=2^n$.

\begin{definition}[Decompressor, describability, complexity]
A \emph{decompressor} is a function $D$ from strings to a set of objects. An object $y$ is \emph{describable} under $D$ if $y=D(p)$ for some string $p$; such a $p$ is a \emph{$D$-program} for $y$. For describable $y$,
\[ K_D(y) \;=\; \min\{\, |p| : D(p)=y \,\}. \]
We use only that $D(p)=y$ implies $K_D(y)\le|p|$, and that a shortest program exists whenever $y$ is describable.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pigeonhole ceiling]\label{def:pigeon}
For any $D$, any $s$, and any finite $T$ with $K_D(y)\le s$ for all $y\in T$, we have $|T|\le 2^{s+1}-1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Seeded / Las Vegas compressor]
A \emph{seeded family} is a map $r\mapsto D_r$ from a finite seed set $R$ to decompressors. The \emph{good-seed set} of $y$ at budget $s$ is
\[ G_s(y) \;=\; \{\, r\in R : y \text{ describable under } D_r \text{ and } K_{D_r}(y)\le s \,\}, \]
and the \emph{success probability} at $y$ is $|G_s(y)|/|R|$ under the uniform seed distribution. The scheme is \emph{Las Vegas}: it never decodes incorrectly, but may fail for unlucky seeds. The \emph{seeded complexity} is $\Kseed(y)=\min\{\,|p| : \exists r\in R,\ D_r(p)=y\,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Search-closed class]
A \emph{search-closed class} $\mathcal C$ consists of a set $\Comp$ of algorithms together with a notion of \emph{allowed fuel bound}, closed under composing an algorithm with a verification test that guards its output, and under bounded search over lengths up to an allowed fuel bound. An algorithm $A$ \emph{inverts} $f$ if $f(A(y))=y$ for every $y$ in the range of $f$. A function $f$ is \emph{honest} if preimage lengths are bounded by an allowed function of $|y|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[One-way function]
$f$ is \emph{one-way in $\mathcal C$} if $f\in\Comp$, $f$ is honest, and no $A\in\Comp$ inverts $f$: for every $A\in\Comp$ there is $y$ in the range of $f$ with $f(A(y))\ne y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Compression tasks]
Let $D$ be a decompressor. An algorithm $A$ is a \emph{shortest-program finder} if for every describable $y$, $D(A(y))=y$ and $|A(y)|=K_D(y)$; a \emph{$\delta$-approximate finder} if instead $|A(y)|\le K_D(y)+\delta(|y|)$. A \emph{prefix-decision oracle} is a predicate $\dectrue(y,w,n)$ intended to answer: \emph{is there a string $p$ of length $n$ with $D(wp)=y$?} A seeded family $\{A_r\}$ with finite seed list $R$ is a \emph{seeded shortest finder} if for every describable $y$ some $r\in R$ has $D(A_r(y))=y$ and $|A_r(y)|=K_D(y)$; a \emph{seeded approximate finder with slack $g$} if instead $|A_r(y)|\le K_D(y)+g(|y|)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[The bridge]
Read a one-way function $f$ as a decompressor: an $f$-program for $y$ \emph{is} a preimage of $y$. A shortest-program finder for $f$ is in particular an inverter of $f$. Every hardness statement below is an instance of this observation.
\end{remark}

\section{Las Vegas compression: the counting theory}\label{sec:count}

\begin{theorem}[Las Vegas double counting]\label{thm:budget}
For every finite seed set $R$, seeded family $\{D_r\}_{r\in R}$, budget $s$, and finite target set $T$,
\[ \sum_{y\in T} |G_s(y)| \;\le\; |R|\,\bigl(2^{s+1}-1\bigr). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $|G_s(y)|=\sum_{r\in R}\mathbf 1[K_{D_r}(y)\le s]$ and exchange the summations. For fixed $r$ the inner sum counts elements of $T$ compressible to $s$ bits by the single decompressor $D_r$, at most $2^{s+1}-1$ by Definition~\ref{def:pigeon}. Summing $|R|$ such bounds gives the claim.
\end{proof}

The theorem is a \emph{budget}: the right-hand side is the supply of (seed, short program) pairs, the left-hand side the demand from $T$. Randomness redistributes description length; it does not manufacture it.

\begin{corollary}[Success-probability law]\label{cor:delta}
If every $y\in T$ is compressed to $s$ bits by at least $m$ seeds, then $m|T|\le|R|(2^{s+1}-1)$, i.e.\ $|T|\le(2^{s+1}-1)/\delta$ with $\delta=m/|R|$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$m|T|=\sum_{y\in T}m\le\sum_{y\in T}|G_s(y)|$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:budget}.
\end{proof}

Equivalently: a Las Vegas compressor with success probability $\delta$ gains at most $\log_2(1/\delta)+1$ bits over the deterministic ceiling. The number of random bits does not appear.

\begin{corollary}[Zero-error randomness gains nothing]\label{cor:zero}
If $R\ne\varnothing$ and every $y\in T$ is compressed to $s$ bits by \emph{every} seed, then $|T|\le 2^{s+1}-1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Corollary~\ref{cor:delta} with $m=|R|$; the positive factor $|R|$ cancels.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Quantified gain]
If $|R|\le m2^{k}$ and every $y\in T$ has at least $m$ good seeds (success probability at least $2^{-k}$), then $|T|\le 2^{k}(2^{s+1}-1)$: the gain over the deterministic ceiling is at most $k$ bits.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Tightness}

Let $R=\{0,1\}^{j}\times\{0,1\}^{i}$ and define the \emph{seeded prefix system} $D_{(u,v)}(p)=u\,p$: the first seed component is prepended to the program, the second is deliberately wasted.

\begin{theorem}[Tightness within one bit]\label{thm:tight}
For all $i,j,s$: every $y$ of length $j+s$ satisfies $|G_s(y)|\ge 2^{i}$ (indeed exactly $2^i$, the good seeds being those whose $u$-component is the length-$j$ prefix of $y$), and
\[ |R|\,(2^{s+1}-1) \;<\; 2\cdot\bigl(2^{i}\cdot|\Bit(j+s)|\bigr). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $u$ is the length-$j$ prefix of $y$, then $p=$ the last $s$ bits of $y$ satisfies $D_{(u,v)}(p)=y$ for all $2^i$ choices of $v$. For the inequality, $|R|=2^{i+j}$ and $|\Bit(j+s)|=2^{j+s}$, so $2^{i+j}(2^{s+1}-1)<2^{i+j}2^{s+1}=2\cdot2^{i}2^{j+s}$.
\end{proof}

The demand exceeds half the budget, so Corollary~\ref{cor:delta} is optimal up to one bit; and the achieved compression depends only on the success probability $2^{-j}$, never on the $i$ extra random bits.

\begin{theorem}[Las Vegas incompressibility]\label{thm:incomp}
For every seeded family over a nonempty finite $R$ and all $s,k$, there is a string $y$ of length $k+s+1$ with $2^{k}|G_s(y)|<|R|$: its success probability is strictly below $2^{-k}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Otherwise $|R|\le 2^k|G_s(y)|$ for all $2^{k+s+1}$ strings $y$ of that length; summing and applying Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} gives $2^{k+s+1}|R|\le 2^{k}|R|(2^{s+1}-1)<2^{k+s+1}|R|$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{Average-case bounds, sharpness, and the seed hierarchy}\label{sec:avg}

\begin{lemma}[Layer-cake]\label{lem:layer}
Let $c:T\to\mathbb N$ on a finite set $T$, let $S\in\mathbb N$, and suppose $|\{y\in T: c(y)\le s\}|\le \mathrm{bnd}(s)$ for every $s<S$. Then
\[ S\,|T| \;\le\; \sum_{y\in T} c(y) \;+\; \sum_{s<S}\mathrm{bnd}(s). \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For each $s$, $|T|=|\{c\le s\}|+|\{c>s\}|$. Sum over $s<S$; the first part is at most $\sum_{s<S}\mathrm{bnd}(s)$, and exchanging summations gives $\sum_{s<S}|\{c>s\}|=\sum_{y\in T}|\{s<S: s<c(y)\}|\le\sum_{y\in T}c(y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Average description length]\label{thm:avg}
For any decompressor $D$ and any finite set $T$ of describable objects with $|T|\ge 2^{n}$,
\[ (n-2)\,|T| \;\le\; \sum_{y\in T} K_D(y). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:layer} with $c=K_D$, $S=n-1$, $\mathrm{bnd}(s)=2^{s+1}-1$. Since $\sum_{s<n-1}(2^{s+1}-1)\le 2^{n}\le|T|$, we get $(n-1)|T|\le\sum_y K_D(y)+|T|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Average description length with randomness]\label{thm:avgseed}
Let $\{D_r\}_{r\in R}$ be a seeded family with $|R|\le 2^{k}$, and $T$ finite with $|T|\ge 2^{n}$, each member describable under some seed. Then
\[ (n-k-3)\,|T| \;\le\; \sum_{y\in T} \Kseed(y). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The seeded ceiling bounds $|\{y: \Kseed(y)\le s\}|$ by $|R|(2^{s+1}-1)\le 2^{k}(2^{s+1}-1)$. Feed this into Lemma~\ref{lem:layer} with $S=n-k-2$; the geometric sum is at most $2^{n-1}\le|T|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of the constant]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $T_m$ be the set of all strings of length at most $m$ and $D$ the identity decompressor, so $K_D(y)=|y|$. Then exactly
\[ \sum_{y\in T_m} K_D(y) + 2\cdot 2^{m+1} \;=\; (m+1)2^{m+1}+2, \qquad |T_m|+1 = 2^{m+1}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$T_m$ is the disjoint union of $\Bit(\ell)$, $\ell\le m$, so $|T_m|=2^{m+1}-1$ and $\sum_y K_D(y)=\sum_{\ell\le m}\ell 2^{\ell}$. The identity $\sum_{\ell<M}\ell2^{\ell}+2\cdot2^{M}=M2^{M}+2$ follows by induction on $M$; take $M=m+1$.
\end{proof}

With $n=m+1$ the average description length is $n-2+o(1)$: Theorem~\ref{thm:avg} cannot be improved beyond an additive $O(1)$.

\begin{theorem}[Strict seed hierarchy]\label{thm:hier}
Fix $s$ and $k\ge1$.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
  \item With $2^{k}$ seeds the prefix system succeeds everywhere: every $y$ of length $k+s$ has a good seed.
  \item With at most $2^{k-1}$ seeds no seeded family achieves this: some $y$ of length $k+s$ has $G_s(y)=\varnothing$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) is Theorem~\ref{thm:tight} with $i=0$, $j=k$. For (2), if every $y\in\Bit(k+s)$ had a good seed, the seeded ceiling gives $2^{k+s}\le|R|(2^{s+1}-1)\le 2^{k-1}(2^{s+1}-1)=2^{k+s}-2^{k-1}$, false since $2^{k-1}>0$.
\end{proof}

Complementing this, a seeded family with $k$-bit seeds is simulated by one deterministic decompressor at additive cost $2k+1$ bits, via a self-delimiting encoding of the seed (its length in unary, then the seed, then the program). Randomness is worth between $k$ and $2k+1$ bits, and nothing outside that window.

\section{The computational boundary}\label{sec:crypto}

\begin{definition}[Las Vegas simulation]
For a seeded family $A$, a target $f$, and a finite seed list $R$,
\[ \mathrm{try}_{f,A,R}(y) = \begin{cases} A_r(y) & \text{for the first } r\in R \text{ with } f(A_r(y))=y,\\ y & \text{if no such } r \text{ exists.}\end{cases} \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Las Vegas class]
A \emph{Las Vegas class} is a search-closed class additionally closed under Las Vegas simulation: if $f\in\Comp$ and every slice $A_r\in\Comp$, then $\mathrm{try}_{f,A,R}\in\Comp$ for every finite list $R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Las Vegas inversion derandomizes]\label{lem:try}
If for every $y$ in the range of $f$ some seed $r\in R$ satisfies $f(A_r(y))=y$, then $\mathrm{try}_{f,A,R}$ inverts $f$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The search for a verifying seed cannot fail (the witness would be found), and whichever seed it returns satisfies the verification predicate by construction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One-way functions defeat Las Vegas algorithms totally]\label{thm:total}
Let $\mathcal C$ be a Las Vegas class and $f$ one-way in $\mathcal C$. For every seeded family $A$ with all slices in $\Comp$ and every finite seed list $R$, there is a $y$ in the range of $f$ with $f(A_r(y))\ne y$ for all $r\in R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Otherwise every $y$ in the range has a good seed, so $\mathrm{try}_{f,A,R}$ inverts $f$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:try} and lies in $\Comp$ by closure---contradicting one-wayness.
\end{proof}

The conclusion is stronger than ``fails with noticeable probability'': on the exhibited input the algorithm fails with probability $1$ over the seed list. This is the computational analogue of Corollary~\ref{cor:zero}.

\begin{corollary}[Las Vegas compression search is blocked]\label{cor:lvsearch}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:total}, no seeded family with slices in $\Comp$ and finite seed list is a seeded shortest finder for $f$ read as a decompressor.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Approximation does not help]\label{cor:approx}
The same holds for seeded approximate finders with \emph{any} additive slack function $g$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch of both]
A seeded finder outputs, for each describable $y$ and some seed, a program $p$ with $f(p)=y$; Theorem~\ref{thm:total} exhibits a $y$ where every seed fails to do even that. Optimality is never used.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Las Vegas inversion $\equiv$ deterministic inversion]
For a Las Vegas class $\mathcal C$: every honest $f\in\Comp$ is inverted by some $A\in\Comp$ if and only if every honest $f\in\Comp$ is inverted by some seeded family with slices in $\Comp$ and a finite seed list.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Forward: take the constant family with a one-element seed list. Backward: Lemma~\ref{lem:try} plus closure.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The characterization]\label{thm:char}
For a Las Vegas class $\mathcal C$ the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
  \item a one-way function for $\mathcal C$ exists;
  \item some honest $D\in\Comp$ has a hard \emph{deterministic} compression-search problem;
  \item some honest $D\in\Comp$ has a hard \emph{Las Vegas} compression-search problem.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1)$\Rightarrow$(3) is Corollary~\ref{cor:lvsearch}; (3)$\Rightarrow$(2) is immediate, a deterministic finder being a one-seed family; (2)$\Rightarrow$(1) follows from the bridge and closure under guarded bounded search: from inverters for all honest functions one builds shortest-program finders by searching lengths upward and verifying.
\end{proof}

\subsection*{Non-vacuity}

The class of \emph{all} functions is a Las Vegas class, contains no one-way function, and in it a single-seed algorithm solves compression search for every honest decompressor---so \S\ref{sec:crypto} is genuinely conditional. The class of \emph{length-nondecreasing} algorithms is also a Las Vegas class (verification never shortens the input, and the fallback branch echoes it) and carries a genuine one-way function, the tagging map $y\mapsto \mathrm{true}\,\|\,y$; there the theorems have real content.

\section{Search-to-decision and local correctness}\label{sec:decision}

Everything above rests on \emph{verification}. The compression dictionary contains one task with no certificate:
\[ \dectrue(y,w,n): \quad \text{is there a string } p \text{ with } |p|=n \text{ and } D(wp)=y? \]
A ``yes'' cannot be checked without solving the problem again, so a Las Vegas decider might plausibly evade Theorem~\ref{thm:total}. It does not: although individual answers are uncheckable, a \emph{sequence} of them composes into a certificate.

\begin{definition}[Reconstruction]
Given a predicate $\dectrue(\cdot,\cdot)$ of a prefix and a remaining length, set $\rebuild(0,w)=w$ and
\[ \rebuild(n+1,w) = \begin{cases} \rebuild(n, w0) & \text{if } \dectrue(w0,n),\\ \rebuild(n, w1) & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases} \]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Correctness of reconstruction]\label{lem:rebuild}
Suppose $\dectrue(w,n)$ holds precisely when some $p$ of length $n$ satisfies $D(wp)=y$. Then for all $n,w$ such that some length-$n$ continuation of $w$ is a $D$-program for $y$, the string $\rebuild(n,w)$ is a $D$-program for $y$ of length $|w|+n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$. For $n=0$ the continuation is empty, so $D(w)=y$. For $n+1$, write the witness as $bt$ with $|t|=n$. If $\dectrue(w0,n)$ holds, descend into $w0$ with a witness supplied by correctness; otherwise the $0$-branch is dead, forcing $b=1$, and $t$ witnesses the $1$-branch. Lengths add.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Decision-to-finder]
With a fuel bound $\mathrm{fuel}(\cdot)$,
\[ \Ffind_{\dectrue}(y) = \rebuild\bigl(n_0(y),\varepsilon\bigr), \qquad n_0(y)=\min\{\, n\le \mathrm{fuel}(|y|) : \dectrue(y,\varepsilon,n) \,\}. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Search-to-decision for compression]\label{thm:s2d}
If $\dectrue$ is a correct prefix-decision oracle for $D$ and $\mathrm{fuel}(|y|)\ge K_D(y)$, then for every describable $y$ we have $D(\Ffind_{\dectrue}(y))=y$ and $|\Ffind_{\dectrue}(y)|=K_D(y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At the empty prefix, $n\mapsto\dectrue(y,\varepsilon,n)$ is the predicate ``$y$ has a program of length $n$'', whose least solution below the fuel bound is $K_D(y)$; then apply Lemma~\ref{lem:rebuild} with $w=\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No efficient prefix decider for a one-way function]
If $f$ is one-way in a search-closed class and $\mathrm{fuel}$ is admissible, then no correct prefix-decision oracle for $f$ can have $\Ffind_{\dectrue}$ in the class---otherwise it would invert $f$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Local correctness}

A Las Vegas decider offers much less than global correctness: for each $y$ some seed is right \emph{about $y$}, and all seeds may be arbitrarily wrong elsewhere.

\begin{definition}[Local correctness]
An oracle $\dectrue$ is \emph{locally correct at $y$} for $D$ if for all $w$ and $n$,
\[ \dectrue(y,w,n)=\text{true} \iff \exists p,\ |p|=n \wedge D(wp)=y. \]
No constraint is placed on $\dectrue(y',\cdot,\cdot)$ for $y'\ne y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Local correctness suffices]\label{thm:local}
If $\dectrue$ is locally correct at $y$, $y$ is describable under $D$, and $\mathrm{fuel}(|y|)\ge K_D(y)$, then $D(\Ffind_{\dectrue}(y))=y$ and $|\Ffind_{\dectrue}(y)|=K_D(y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Define the \emph{globalization}
\[ \widehat{\dectrue}(y',w,n) = \begin{cases} \dectrue(y,w,n) & y'=y,\\ [\exists p,\ |p|=n \wedge D(wp)=y'] & y'\ne y. \end{cases} \]
By local correctness at $y$ it is globally correct, so Theorem~\ref{thm:s2d} applies to it. But $\Ffind$ evaluated at $y$ queries only the $y$-slice of the oracle, and $\widehat{\dectrue}(y,\cdot,\cdot)=\dectrue(y,\cdot,\cdot)$ by construction: the two runs are the same computation. Transport the conclusion along that equality.
\end{proof}

The globalization is a purely mathematical device; it need not be effective, and no algorithm ever constructs it.

\begin{theorem}[Las Vegas decision oracles yield an inverter]\label{thm:lvdec}
Let $\{\dectrue_r\}_{r\in R}$ be a family of decision oracles indexed by a finite seed list, and $\mathrm{fuel}$ a bound with $\mathrm{fuel}(|y|)\ge K_f(y)$ for every describable $y$. If for every $y$ in the range of $f$ some $\dectrue_r$ is locally correct at $y$, then $\mathrm{try}_{f,\,(r\mapsto\Ffind_{\dectrue_r}),\,R}$ inverts $f$ deterministically.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix $y$ and a locally correct seed $r$. Theorem~\ref{thm:local} makes $\Ffind_{\dectrue_r}(y)$ a genuine $f$-program for $y$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:try} applies.
\end{proof}

The pivot: \emph{the oracle's answers need not be checkable---only the reconstructed program is, and that suffices.}

\begin{theorem}[One-way functions defeat Las Vegas deciders]\label{thm:owfdec}
Let $\mathcal C$ be a Las Vegas class, $f$ one-way in $\mathcal C$, $\mathrm{fuel}$ admissible as above, and $\{\dectrue_r\}$ a seeded oracle family whose induced finders all lie in $\Comp$. Then for every finite seed list $R$ there is a $y$ in the range of $f$ at which \emph{every} seed's oracle fails to be locally correct.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:total} to the family $r\mapsto\Ffind_{\dectrue_r}$ to get a describable $y$ on which all seeds fail to output a preimage; local correctness of any seed at $y$ would contradict this via Theorem~\ref{thm:local}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Decision is at least as hard as search]
A seeded family of locally correct decision oracles yields a seeded shortest-program finder: for every describable $y$, some seed's reconstruction outputs a program of length exactly $K_D(y)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The obstruction is computational, not informational}\label{sec:ideal}

\begin{definition}[Ideal oracle]
For a decompressor $D$ let $\dectrue^{\ast}_D(y,w,n)$ be true precisely when some $p$ of length $n$ satisfies $D(wp)=y$. This is well defined for every $D$, effective or not.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[No information-theoretic obstruction]\label{thm:ideal}
For every $D$ and admissible fuel bound, $\Ffind_{\dectrue^{\ast}_D}$ is an exact shortest-program finder for $D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\dectrue^{\ast}_D$ is globally correct by definition; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:s2d}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The Las Vegas dictionary]\label{thm:dict}
Let $\mathcal C$ be a Las Vegas class, $f$ one-way in $\mathcal C$, $\mathrm{fuel}$ admissible, $A$ a seeded family with slices in $\Comp$, $R$ a finite seed list, $g$ any slack function, and $\{\dectrue_r\}$ a seeded oracle family with all induced finders in $\Comp$. Then simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
  \item there is a describable $y$ with $f(A_r(y))\ne y$ for all $r\in R$;
  \item $A$ is not a seeded shortest finder for $f$;
  \item $A$ is not a seeded approximate finder with slack $g$, for any $g$;
  \item there is a describable $y$ at which no $\dectrue_r$ is locally correct;
\end{enumerate}
and yet
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]\setcounter{enumi}{4}
  \item $\Ffind_{\dectrue^{\ast}_f}$ is an exact shortest-program finder for $f$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

Items 1--4 are four faces of one cryptographic barrier; item 5 certifies that the barrier is about computation, not about the existence of the information.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Las Vegas simulation.} For each $r\in R$ in order, compute $c\leftarrow A_r(y)$ and test $f(c)=y$; return the first $c$ that passes, else $y$. Cost: $|R|$ slice invocations and $|R|$ evaluations of $f$. Correctness: Lemma~\ref{lem:try}. This procedure is what makes Las Vegas randomness worthless against verifiable tasks.

\paragraph{B. Prefix reconstruction.} Set $w\leftarrow\varepsilon$ and repeat $n$ times: if $\dectrue(y,w0,\text{remaining}-1)$ then $w\leftarrow w0$ else $w\leftarrow w1$; return $w$. Cost: exactly $n$ oracle queries, one per output bit. Correctness: Lemma~\ref{lem:rebuild} globally, Theorem~\ref{thm:local} locally. Composed with a bounded search for the least feasible length it becomes an exact shortest-program finder.

\paragraph{C. Good-seed census.} For each $y\in T$, enumerate programs of length at most $s$ under each seed and record $|G_s(y)|$; compare $\sum_y|G_s(y)|$ with $|R|(2^{s+1}-1)$, the minimum success probability with $\log_2(1/\delta)+1$, and the seeded complexities with the average-case bounds. Cost: $O(|R|\,2^{s+1})$ decodings. This is the experimental counterpart of Theorems~\ref{thm:budget}--\ref{thm:incomp} and \ref{thm:avg}--\ref{thm:hier}.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{What randomness is worth.} Informationally, exactly $\log_2(1/\delta)$ bits, where $\delta$ is the tolerated failure probability---in the worst case (Corollary~\ref{cor:delta}), on average (Theorem~\ref{thm:avgseed}), tightly (Theorem~\ref{thm:tight}) and rigidly (Theorem~\ref{thm:hier}); it does not depend on the entropy consumed, and vanishes if failure is disallowed (Corollary~\ref{cor:zero}). Computationally, nothing at all (Theorems~\ref{thm:total}, \ref{thm:char}, \ref{thm:owfdec}).

\paragraph{A design rule.} A compressor promising to fail at most once in $2^{10}$ tries may hope for about $11$ extra bits over the deterministic ceiling and no more, whatever its seed length. Lengthening the seed is misdirected effort; relaxing the success requirement is the only lever.

\paragraph{A warning to optimizers.} Any procedure reliably finding shortest---or near-shortest, or merely valid---descriptions for an honest decompressor inverts that decompressor read as a function. Since Corollary~\ref{cor:approx} permits arbitrary slack, this is not about optimality but about ever producing a correct description. Universal compression is universal cryptanalysis.

\paragraph{Scope.} Our notion of one-wayness is worst-case, uniform and deterministic, and seed sets are finite lists rather than parametrized distributions. This minimality keeps the arguments combinatorial and lets them transfer upward to stronger hardness notions; the price is that they say nothing directly about distributional compression.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[The average-case randomness slack is $1$, not $3$]
For every seeded family with $|R|\le 2^{k}$ and every finite $T$ with $|T|\ge2^{n}$ whose members are describable under some seed, $(n-k-1)|T|\le\sum_{y\in T}\Kseed(y)$; and $1$ cannot be replaced by $0$. Theorem~\ref{thm:avgseed} gives $n-k-3$, while numerical data for the prefix families indicate $n-k-1$. The layer-cake argument discards the geometric tail in one lump; a rank-based argument---order $T$ by seeded complexity and charge the $i$-th element at least $\log_2(i/|R|)$---should lose only the single bit inherent in $2^{s+1}-1$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Verification, not randomness, collapses Las Vegas to deterministic]
There is a Las Vegas class $\mathcal C$, a function $f$ one-way for $\mathcal C$, and a seeded family with slices in $\mathcal C$ and only two seeds, such that for every $y$ at least one seed outputs the \emph{number} $K_f(y)$ correctly---even though no seeded family can output a \emph{program}. The simulation derandomizes precisely because search answers are checkable; for tasks whose output carries no certificate a genuine Las Vegas/deterministic gap should be exhibitable. Theorem~\ref{thm:owfdec} shows the prefix decision task still collapses because its answers compose into a checkable program, so isolating the smallest non-composable task would pin down where randomness can live.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Distributional compression $\equiv$ distributional one-wayness]
For a Las Vegas class closed under sampling, the following are equivalent: (i) some honest $f$ is distributionally one-way---no class algorithm outputs a preimage for more than a $1-1/|T|$ fraction of $T=f(\Bit(n))$ for infinitely many $n$; (ii) some honest decompressor $D$ admits no class algorithm outputting shortest $D$-programs for more than a $1-1/|T|$ fraction of the same set.
\end{conjecture}

Further targets: tightening the derandomization window $[k,2k+1]$; extending the search-to-decision bridge to \emph{conditional} complexity, where the prefix carries side information; and quantifying how total failure degrades when the seed list grows with the input length.

\section{Conclusion}

The pigeonhole principle bounds how many objects can have short descriptions; cryptographic hardness bounds how many of those descriptions can be found. The distance between the two bounds, in the presence of randomness, is exactly $\log_2(1/\delta)$ bits on the information side and exactly zero on the computation side. The technical heart is a single observation about certificates: randomness collapses to determinism whenever a candidate answer can be checked. The decision version of compressibility appears to escape this, admitting no certificate---but its answers, taken along a descending chain of prefixes, assemble into a program, and a program can be run.

